WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:20.029
Fairfax turned out to be exactly as she appeared: a placid woman of average intelligence.

2
00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.854
I was thankful for the kindness she showed me, and I took pleasure in spending time with her.

3
00:00:26.974 --> 00:00:30.437
Adele turned out to be a lively, spoiled child.

4
00:00:31.558 --> 00:00:36.321
She had no extraordinary talents, but she made reasonable progress with her studies.

5
00:00:37.521 --> 00:00:42.803
Her efforts to please me inspired affection, and so we were content in each other's company.

6
00:00:43.863 --> 00:00:48.464
But sometimes when I went down to the gates and looked out at the road or climbed up

7
00:00:48.504 --> 00:00:53.206
to the roof and gazed out over the fields, I hungered for something more.

8
00:00:54.446 --> 00:00:56.787
I valued what was good in Adele and Mrs.

9
00:00:56.847 --> 00:01:02.889
Fairfax, but I longed to meet a greater variety of people and to see places that I had

10
00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:03.710
only heard of.

11
00:01:04.891 --> 00:01:06.512
Restlessness was in my nature.

12
00:01:07.673 --> 00:01:10.716
Women need to keep their minds active as much as men do.

13
00:01:12.477 --> 00:01:17.461
It is thoughtless to condemn them if they seek to do more or learn more than custom has

14
00:01:17.501 --> 00:01:19.202
deemed appropriate for their sex.

15
00:01:20.343 --> 00:01:23.786
My sole relief was to walk along the third-floor corridor.

16
00:01:24.887 --> 00:01:30.932
Safe in the silence and solitude, I let my mind dwell on whatever bright visions rose before it.

17
00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.721
When I was alone there, I often heard Grace Poole's laugh.

18
00:01:36.882 --> 00:01:41.583
I also heard her eccentric murmurs, which were even stranger than her laugh.

19
00:01:42.823 --> 00:01:47.125
Sometimes she would come out of her room with a plate or tray and go down to the

20
00:01:47.145 --> 00:01:54.227
kitchen. I made a few attempts to draw her into conversation, but her short replies ended my efforts.

21
00:01:55.507 --> 00:01:58.228
October, November, and December passed.

22
00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:04.015
One day in January, we didn't have lessons because Adele was feeling ill.

23
00:02:05.136 --> 00:02:09.438
It was a fine, cold day, and I grew tired of sitting inside.

24
00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:16.343
Mrs. Fairfax had written a letter, so I offered to carry it to the post office in Hay.

25
00:02:17.443 --> 00:02:21.706
The distance—two miles—would make a pleasant late-afternoon walk.

26
00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:24.988
It was uphill all the way to Hay.

27
00:02:26.088 --> 00:02:29.731
When I was about halfway there, I stopped to rest on a fence.

28
00:02:30.972 --> 00:02:34.295
From my seat I could look down on the gray house in the distance.

29
00:02:35.616 --> 00:02:40.700
I lingered there until the sun went down among the trees and the moon began to rise.

30
00:02:42.001 --> 00:02:46.024
Suddenly I heard the sound of hoofbeats—a horse was coming.

31
00:02:47.225 --> 00:02:52.350
I remembered one of Bessie's tales about a spirit in the shape of a horse or large dog

32
00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:54.952
that sometimes haunted weary travelers.

33
00:02:56.136 --> 00:03:00.397
Just then a great black-and-white dog rushed silently past me!

34
00:03:01.557 --> 00:03:05.318
The horse followed, a tall steed with a rider on its back.

35
00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:11.920
No one ever rode the creature in Bessie's tale, so this was no spirit, only a traveler.

36
00:03:13.581 --> 00:03:16.461
Horse and rider passed by, and I stood up.

37
00:03:17.642 --> 00:03:22.683
I had only gone a few steps when I heard a sliding sound and a clattering tumble.

38
00:03:22.703 --> 00:03:24.604
"What now?"

39
00:03:25.084 --> 00:03:25.804
cried the man.

40
00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:31.493
Man and horse were down on the ground, having slipped on the ice that glazed the road.

41
00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:34.334
The dog came bounding back.

42
00:03:35.474 --> 00:03:41.836
Seeing his master in a predicament and hearing the horse groan, the dog barked until the hills echoed

43
00:03:41.876 --> 00:03:42.616
with the sound.

44
00:03:43.777 --> 00:03:48.218
Then he ran up to me for help because I was the only person nearby.

45
00:03:49.378 --> 00:03:54.840
I followed the dog over to the traveler, who was struggling to work himself free from his horse.

46
00:03:56.084 --> 00:03:59.867
His efforts were so vigorous that I didn't think he could be badly hurt.

47
00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:01.989
"Are you injured, sir?"

48
00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.792
The man was still concentrating on freeing himself.

49
00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:07.694
I spoke again.

50
00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:09.856
"Can I do anything?"

51
00:04:09.876 --> 00:04:16.741
"You must stand aside," he said as he rose first to his knees and then to his feet.

52
00:04:18.022 --> 00:04:24.628
I did so, whereupon the horse began the heaving, stamping, clattering process of getting to its feet.

53
00:04:25.856 --> 00:04:28.217
All the while the dog barked and bayed.

54
00:04:28.237 --> 00:04:30.317
"Quiet, Pilot!"

55
00:04:30.697 --> 00:04:33.198
the man said, and the dog went silent.

56
00:04:34.279 --> 00:04:37.439
The traveler bent over and felt his own foot and leg.

57
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.621
Apparently something was wrong because he now sat down on the fence that I had just left.

58
00:04:44.721 --> 00:04:45.642
I approached him again.

59
00:04:45.662 --> 00:04:52.824
"If you are hurt and want help, sir," I said, "I can fetch someone from Thornfield or from

60
00:04:52.904 --> 00:04:55.712
Hay." "Thank you, but I'll manage.

61
00:04:56.753 --> 00:04:59.013
I have no broken bones, only a sprain."

62
00:05:00.154 --> 00:05:01.974
He stood up and tried his foot again.

63
00:05:01.994 --> 00:05:04.775
"Ugh!" he groaned.

64
00:05:06.616 --> 00:05:09.976
Some daylight still lingered, and I could plainly see him.

65
00:05:11.137 --> 00:05:16.918
He had a dark face with stern features and black hair and looked to be in his mid-thirties.

66
00:05:18.059 --> 00:05:22.240
Had he been a handsome young gentleman, I wouldn't have dared speak to him.

67
00:05:23.472 --> 00:05:27.694
If he had cheerfully refused my offers of assistance, I would have gone on my way.

68
00:05:28.874 --> 00:05:30.715
But his frown made me stay.

69
00:05:30.735 --> 00:05:36.398
"I cannot think of leaving you at so late an hour on this lonely road until you're fit

70
00:05:36.438 --> 00:05:37.378
to ride your horse."

71
00:05:38.599 --> 00:05:43.021
He looked at me when I said this; he'd hardly turned his eyes in my direction before.

72
00:05:43.041 --> 00:05:46.762
"I'd think you should be at home yourself," he said.

73
00:05:46.782 --> 00:05:48.843
"Where do you come from?"

74
00:05:48.863 --> 00:05:53.606
"From just below, and I'm not afraid of being out late when there's moonlight."

75
00:05:53.626 --> 00:05:56.648
"Do you mean you live at Thornfield?"

76
00:05:58.509 --> 00:05:59.570
He pointed to the house.

77
00:06:00.711 --> 00:06:01.191
"Yes, sir."

78
00:06:01.211 --> 00:06:03.272
"Whose house is that?"

79
00:06:03.292 --> 00:06:06.294
"Mr. Rochester's," I replied.

80
00:06:06.314 --> 00:06:08.256
"Do you know him?"

81
00:06:08.276 --> 00:06:10.758
"No, I've never seen him."

82
00:06:10.778 --> 00:06:14.200
"You're not a servant at Thornfield, of course.

83
00:06:15.561 --> 00:06:18.263
You are He studied me intently.

84
00:06:18.283 --> 00:06:19.904
"The governess."

85
00:06:21.468 --> 00:06:24.570
"Ah! I'd forgotten about the governess," he said.

86
00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:27.352
"You may help me if you'll be so kind.

87
00:06:28.372 --> 00:06:31.274
Try to get hold of my horse's bridle and lead him to me."

88
00:06:32.254 --> 00:06:34.636
But try as I might, I couldn't catch the animal.

89
00:06:35.636 --> 00:06:41.920
And so with a slight apology, the man stood up, leaned heavily upon me, and limped over to

90
00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:42.480
his horse.

91
00:06:43.621 --> 00:06:46.142
He climbed into the saddle with a wince of pain.

92
00:06:47.303 --> 00:06:49.684
I handed him his whip, and off he rode.

93
00:06:51.516 --> 00:06:56.737
After taking the letter to Hay, I was reluctant to go home because I'd had this small adventure.

94
00:06:57.818 --> 00:07:00.879
To return to Thornfield was to return to stagnation.

95
00:07:01.999 --> 00:07:07.260
So I lingered at the gates, and then I lingered on the lawn, watching the moon and the

96
00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:12.722
stars. At last I had to turn away from the sky and enter the house.

97
00:07:13.902 --> 00:07:14.802
When I went to Mrs.

98
00:07:14.862 --> 00:07:19.284
Fairfax's room, no one was there except a great black-and-white dog.

99
00:07:19.304 --> 00:07:23.922
"Pilot?" I said, and the dog came to me.

100
00:07:25.023 --> 00:07:26.844
I rang the bell and Leah entered.

101
00:07:26.864 --> 00:07:28.906
"Whose dog is this?"

102
00:07:29.406 --> 00:07:29.947
I asked.

103
00:07:31.007 --> 00:07:33.189
"Mr. Rochester's," Leah replied.

104
00:07:33.209 --> 00:07:36.532
"John just went for the doctor because Mr.

105
00:07:36.632 --> 00:07:38.233
Rochester sprained his ankle.

106
00:07:39.314 --> 00:07:40.855
His horse fell on some ice."
